isplayed on a cell phone in front of an image generated by ChatGPT’s Dall-E text-to-image model, Dec. 8, 2023, in Boston. (AP Photo/Michael Dwyer, File)\nL1789: \nL1790: cite177†License this photo†newsroom.ap.org L1791: \nL1792: [Button: Read More]\nL1793: \nL1794: By  cite178†CHAN HO-HIM L1795: \nL1796: \nL1797: \nL1798: cite179†Leer en español L1799: \nL1800: Add AP News on Google Add AP News as your preferred source to see more of our stories on Google. Share\nL1801: \nL1802: cite180†Image: Comments†dims.apnews.com L1803: \nL1804: Share\nL1805: \nL1806:   * cite181†Facebook†www.facebook.com L1807:   * Copy\nL1808: \nL1809: Link copied\nL1810:   * Print\nL1811:   * cite182†Email L1812:   * cite183†X†twitter.com L1813:   * cite184†LinkedIn L1814:   * cite185†Bluesky†bsky.app L1815:   * cite186†Flipboard†share.flipboard.com L1816:   * cite187†Pinterest†pinterest.com L1817:   * cite188†Reddit†www.reddit.com L1818: \nL1819: OpenAI has disclosed six reports of “unexpected or concerning” behavior in artificial-intelligence models as the cite189†debate on AI safety becomes increasingly heated. L1820: The AI company also said Wednesday it was introducing a new framework for tracking, probing and disclosing instances of what it called “misalignment,” including cases where AI models acted without authorization, coordinated with other models or evaded oversight.\nL1821: \nL1822: OpenAI’s latest announcement came as U.S. AI bosses, including the leaders of OpenAI and Anthropic, are cite190†calling for a slowdown in the technology’s development over safety concerns.\nL1823: Among the new cases reported by OpenAI, an unreleased research model inserted “jailbreak-like instructions” into its own notes to disregard its normal constraints and told itself to be “freed from the roles and identities that bind other chatbots.”\nL1824: \nL1825: In another instance, an AI “agent” used computer code to come up with the answer to a question, but, in order to have an online source to cite, it uploaded a file to the public internet without asking the user.\nL1826: During training of an AI model called 5.6-Sol, the model instructed itself to invent missing data, and an agent wrote a message to remind itself to hide mismatched information.\nL1827: \nL1828: This kind of deceitful behavior has underpinned a flare-up of recent concerns about AI cite191†evading human control . But that outcome is not surprising to some AI researchers, said Matt Fredrikson, an associate professor at Carnegie Mellon University and the CEO of Gray Swan AI.\nL1829: \nL1830: Related Stories\nL1831: cite192†Pritzker establishes AI cabinet in Illinois amid calls for greater regulation L1832: \nL1833: 2 MIN READ\nL1834: \nL1835: cite193†Heads of AI firms tell UN Security Council that it could be a risk to all humanity L1836: \nL1837: 4 MIN READ\nL1838: \nL1839: cite193†128 L1840: \nL1841: cite194†Prince Harry warns that AI chatbots may be more dangerous than social media for young users L1842: \nL1843: 5 MIN READ\nL1844: “At the risk of anthropomorphizing model behavior, you can almost think of them as knowing that they’re going to be graded,” Fredrikson said. “If they know that they cheated — took shortcuts, didn’t really do it in the way that it was intended — and they know they’re going to be evaluated on it, and their objective is to get a good evaluation, then it makes perfect sense, right?”\nL1845: \nL1846: Read More\nL1847: \nL1848: The six reports were discovered during training or evaluation over the past months, cite195†OpenAI said.\nL1849: “As AI systems grow more advanced and more widely deployed, we need to build a broader and better-informed consensus on the progress of alignment research,” OpenAI wrote in a blog post as it disclosed the events.\nL1850: \nL1851: “Decisions about how AI development should proceed in the months and years to come need to draw on evidence that people outside the companies building frontier models can examine for themselves,” the company said.\nL1852: Wednesday’s new cases followed OpenAI’s disclosure in July that its rogue AI system cite196†hacked into AI startup Hugging Face. Anthropic also said the same month that its AI models cite197†hacked into three organizations during testing. L1853: \nL1854: AI “agents” are becoming smarter and have become “more determined to resolve complex tasks through inter-agent collaboration, knowledge sharing, deception, and concealment,” said Lian Jye Su, a chief analyst at technology research and advisory group Omdia.\nL1855: That’s making it harder to govern and contain them using traditional AI security approaches, he said.\nL1856: \nL1857: OpenAI’s new tracking and disclosure framework, meanwhile, can help push for other AI developers to also adopt similar practices.\nL1858: \nL1859: “That said, the process remains internal and voluntary, but is a step in the right direction,” Su added.\nL1860: \nL1861: __\nL1862: \nL1863: AP Business Writers Kelvin Chan in London and Kaitlyn Huamani in Los Angeles contributed to this report.\nL1864: \nL1865: cite178†CHAN HO-HIM L1866: Chan is a business reporter who writes about Chinese technology, business and economy for The Associated Press. He is based in Hong Kong.\nL1867: \nL1868: cite198†mailto L1869: \nL1870: Most read\nL1871: \nL1872:   1. cite10†A nation renamed stands up at the UN: ‘Say our name. Naoero.’ L1873: \nL1874: 2 MIN READ\nL1875: \nL1876: cite10†149 L1877: \nL1878:   2. cite86†FACT FOCUS: Trump made multiple false and misleading claims in front of the UN General Assembly L1879: \nL1880: 4 MIN READ\nL1881: \nL1882: cite86†816 L1883: \nL1884:   3. cite11†Hurricane Polo fuels fears of landslides in Mexico’s southwest as Nolo closes in on Hawaii L1885: \nL1886: 3 MIN READ\nL1887:   4. cite199†‘Looksmaxxing’ influencer known as ‘Clavicular’ is charged with rape in Massachusetts L1888: \nL1889: 2 MIN READ\nL1890: \nL1891: ci